# Day 3: Library Helpdesk Assistant in LangChain

## Goal
Rebuild the helpdesk chatbot with LangChain building blocks (prompt template, memory, chain), test it with real questions, and get a first look at an agent. This day uses **Google Gemini** (a non-Groq provider).

## What this notebook covers
| Section | What it shows |
|---|---|
| Setup | `ChatGoogleGenerativeAI` with the Gemini API key from Colab Secrets (`SECRET_NAME`) |
| Template + memory + chain | `ChatPromptTemplate` with a `MessagesPlaceholder("history")`, and the chain `prompt \| llm \| StrOutputParser()` |
| `invoke_with_retry()` | Retries the call if Gemini is busy (503) or rate limited per minute (429) |
| Tests | `tests.csv` with 10 questions and a `must_include` check for each, run by `run_tests()` to get a pass rate |
| Improve | Change one thing in the prompt, re-run the tests, and compare the pass rate |
| Switch | Temperature 1.0 run twice to see if results change between runs |
| Agent sneak peek | `create_agent` with an empty tool list (tools come on Day 4) |
| Agent or not | Three library jobs labelled plain code, one LLM call, or agent |
| Before Day 4 | Two functions with type hints and docstrings: `late_fine()` and `is_available()` |

## Key ideas
- Chatbot vs agent: an answer-giver vs something that acts to reach a goal
- A template has blanks in `{ }`, and a chain connects `prompt | llm | parser`
- Tests are real questions plus a word the answer must include, and are re-run after every change
- Use the simplest option that works (plain code, one LLM call, or an agent)

